Trace: the only route into BaseLPLib.finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest is _redeemShares(isEscrowed=true) →
  _withdrawPendingRequests, and escrowed redemption only applies to an account that already holds a pending withdraw
  request (_isWithdrawRequestPending → hasPendingWithdrawals). That state can only be created by __initiateWithdraw →
  BaseLPLib.initiateWithdraw, which exits the pool proportionally (isSingleSided: false) and then, for every leg with a
  non‑zero exit balance, does ADDRESS_REGISTRY.getWithdrawRequestManager(tokens[i]) followed by
  tokens[i].checkApprove(address(manager), …) and manager.initiateWithdraw(...) — with no zero-manager guard either. So
  on a two-token Curve pool where one leg has no registered manager, the proportional exit yields a non-zero balance on
  that leg and initiateWithdraw reverts (approve/call into address(0)) before any request is ever recorded. No account
  can end up escrowed with a request on one leg and a zero manager on the other; the "one-legged request state" the
  claim depends on is not reachable through the code's own entry point. The same all-legs-have-requests premise is baked
  into getWithdrawRequestValue, which require(hasRequest) for every token — the design uniformly assumes each pool
  token has a manager, and whether a manager is registered is controlled by ADDRESS_REGISTRY (a
  privileged/governance-controlled registry), so the only way to produce the claimed asymmetry is a misconfigured
  deployment, not an unprivileged action. The residual sub-case (both managers registered but one leg's proportional
  exit balance is exactly 0, so initiateWithdraw skips it and w.sharesAmount == 0 later divides by zero) is not
  demonstrated to be reachable for a real Curve 2-token pool and is not the exploit as filed. Per the tie-break, no
  unambiguous unprivileged path exists.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/AbstractSingleSidedLP.sol:finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest|class=C15|The escrowed
  state the claim needs is unreachable: BaseLPLib.initiateWithdraw itself has no zero-manager guard and reverts on the
  proportional (non-zero) exit balance of any leg lacking a manager, so no withdraw request can ever be recorded in the
  "one-legged" configuration, which in any case is a privileged ADDRESS_REGISTRY/deployment misconfiguration rather than
  an unprivileged attack.
